depositors, depending on market conditions.
Permitted Activities and Investments by Financial Holding Companies
The BHC Act generally prohibits a financial holding company from engaging, directly or indirectly, in activities other than banking or managing or controlling
banks, except for activities determined by the Federal Reserve to be so closely related to banking or managing or controlling banks as to be a proper incident thereto.
Activities that the Federal Reserve Board has found to be so closely related to banking as to be a proper incident to the business of banking include:
● factoring accounts receivable;
● making, acquiring, brokering or servicing loans and usual related activities;
● leasing personal or real property;
● operating a nonbank depository institution, such as a savings association;
● trust company functions;
● financial and investment advisory activities;
● conducting discount securities brokerage activities;
